
/**
 * fat_arg_builtin_arity — how many LEADING by-value `tk_str` parameters does the
 * C entry `sym` declare? The ABI-arity table the x86 isel reads to decide how
 * many flattened `(ptr, len)` pairs it must re-materialize and pass BY REFERENCE
 * on an ABI that carries no 16-byte aggregate in a register
 * (`isel_x86_64.tks::by_ref_fat_pairs_x86`).
 *
 * IT SPANS BOTH HAND-WRITTEN C SEEDS, and that is the whole point: this
 * repository has TWO of them, `src/runtime/teko_rt.{c,h}` and
 * `src/assert/assert.{c,h}`, and BOTH declare by-value `tk_str` parameters. The
 * first round of this work swept only the runtime, which left every
 * `teko::assert::<name>` call passing the flat pair to a callee that expects a
 * pointer — measured as `own_native`'s ACCESS_VIOLATION on its FIRST case
 * (2026-07-30, the corpus's `scenario()` harness opens with
 * `teko::assert::scenario_begin(name)`). The assert half is DERIVED from
 * `checker::assert_fat_arg_arity`, i.e. from the family's one signature table,
 * so a new assertion taking a `str` is covered the day it is declared.
 *
 * ONE / TWO — `ONE_STR_BY_VALUE_ENTRIES` / `TWO_STR_BY_VALUE_ENTRIES`, each a faithful
 * transcription of `teko_rt.h` rather than of the subset some lowering names today, plus the
 * `teko__assert__` half derived from the checker's signature table.
 *
 * ZERO — everything else, and the interesting half of "everything else" is the
 * family that was FLATTENED IN THE RUNTIME on purpose (`tk_str_concat_len`,
 * `tk_str_slice_len`, `tk_str_of_bytes_len`, `tk_slice_str_eq`, … — each
 * declaring `(const tk_byte *ptr, uint64_t len, …)` rather than a `tk_str`). Those
 * take no by-value aggregate at all, so the flat pair IS their C ABI on EVERY
 * target and this function must keep answering 0 for them.
 *
 * LEADING, AND THE HEADER HAS EXACTLY THREE ENTRIES THAT ARE NOT. `tk_fmt_dyn_f64`/
 * `tk_fmt_dyn_i64`/`tk_fmt_dyn_u64` take `(scalar, tk_str spec)`, so their by-value aggregate is
 * the SECOND parameter and no leading count can describe them. No lowering names them — every
 * dynamic-format call goes to the out-parameter twin `tk_fmt_dyn_*_len`, whose `spec` is already
 * the flat `(ptr, len)` and is therefore right on every ABI (verified in the emitted
 * `x86_64-windows` object: the relocations name only the `_len` spelling). Should one ever be
 * named directly, this table would silently answer 0; the honest move then is a stop, not a
 * widened count.
 *
 * WHY AN ARITY AND NOT A PREDICATE. On SysV/AAPCS64/LP64D the two spellings are
 * the SAME ABI (a 2-eightbyte aggregate decomposes into two consecutive integer
 * argument registers), which is exactly why passing `tk_str_eq` the flat
 * four-operand form is invisible on Linux and macOS and faults with
 * ACCESS_VIOLATION (0xC0000005) on Windows — measured, `own_native`'s
 * `own_arith_exit` and `regressor`'s `alias_fat_field`, both without writing a
 * byte. The former single-`tk_str` fix keyed on a two-operand argument list and
 * was structurally unable to reach a call carrying TWO pairs.
 *
 * @param str sym  the resolved C symbol (`call_symbol`'s result, or one of
 *                 the hardcoded symbols this module emits)
 * @return u64  the count of leading by-value `tk_str` parameters (0, 1 or 2)
 */
pub fn fat_arg_builtin_arity(sym: str) -> u64 {
    if takes_one_str_by_value(sym) { return 1 }
    if takes_two_strs_by_value(sym) { return 2 }
    assert_seed_fat_arity(sym)
}
pub type LoweredArgs = struct { ctx: LowerCtx; vregs: []u32 }
